            <ArticleTitle>The Asthma Predictive Index: early diagnosis of asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>157-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/ACI.0b013e3283464c4a</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE OF REVIEW" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Recurrent wheezing is a common problem in young children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RECENT FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Approximately 40% of children wheeze in their first year of life. However, only 30% of preschoolers with recurrent wheezing still have asthma at the age of 6 years. Nevertheless, asthma, the most prevalent chronic disease in children, is difficult to diagnose in infants and preschoolers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We review the importance of determining at early age which infant/preschoolers will develop asthma later on life and discuss the utility of the Asthma Predictive Index.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
